{
 "interest": "Documents US scrutiny of pre-embargo Japanese exports, culminating in 1941 evidence of Japan secretly building naval bases across Pacific Mandates.",
 "keywords": [
  "moral embargo",
  "aviation gasoline",
  "petroleum technology",
  "scrap iron",
  "Nanyo Kohatsu Kaisha",
  "Japanese Mandated Islands",
  "Universal Oil Products",
  "American Committee for Non-Participation in Japanese Aggression",
  "strategic materials",
  "aircraft export restrictions",
  "trans-Siberian shipments",
  "southward expansion",
  "synthetic fuel plants"
 ],
 "overview": "Decimal file 894.24 (Equipment and Supplies - Japan) documents the United States government's administration, inter-agency coordination, and public debate concerning the export of war materials, refining technology, and aeronautical equipment to Japan between late 1939 and mid-1941. Pages 1 through 24 contain the State Department's internal docket register ('List of Papers', Form DS-46, sheets 92 through 114), which chronologically abstracts incoming and outgoing correspondence, corporate inquiries, and diplomatic memoranda regarding the enforcement of the U.S. 'moral embargo' on high-octane aviation fuel technology, scrap iron, molybdenum, and airplane parts, as well as extensive congressional and citizen agitation led by the American Committee for Non-Participation in Japanese Aggression. The file concludes on pages 25–26 with an April 23, 1941 Division of Far Eastern Affairs memorandum summarizing British intelligence from corporate archives seized by the Netherlands East Indies government at Manokwari, revealing extensive clandestine military fortifications, oil tank installations, and harbor works undertaken across the Mandated Islands, Hainan, and the Spratlys by the Japanese Nanyo Development Company (Nanyo Kohatsu Kaisha).",
